From 40a158bb657ab820b3d87203f58239350328ad17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:09:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): a mandatory model pin rides the worker pool; the
 claiming worker runs exactly it or refuses [daveey]

A card created with kanban_create(model=..., pin_mandatory=true) never reached a pool worker. An example is
t_01a1cefa6145100f, an Opus exact-head review that stayed on the hub with `option: pin_mandatory=1`.
Pool portability (kanban_placement.portable_reason) reused the courier's UNCARRIED_OPTIONS, and that list
names pin_mandatory, so every such card got `uncarried option`. The pool route never used the courier.
model_override, provider_override and model_pin_mandatory are Board row fields (kanban_db_sync.SYNCED_COLUMNS)
that a worker's pickup already copies.

Five whys
1. Why did the Opus review stay on the hub? portable_reason returned `uncarried option`.
2. Why? pin_mandatory is in UNCARRIED_OPTIONS, and pool portability checks that list.
3. Why is it in that list? `hermes-hub create-task` (the cross-gateway courier) cannot carry the mark. The list
   was written for the courier, and pool portability borrowed it whole.
4. Why was that wrong for the pool? The pool replicates the card row, mark included, so the pin was already
   transported. The only real gap was on the worker side: nothing stopped a worker from running a mandatory pin
   it could not serve. route_model_mismatch -> clear_refused_pin would even drop the pin for the profile default.
5. Why did no test catch it? The placement tests covered the courier options, and the pin-reroute tests covered
   the quota path. No test followed a mandatory pin through pool enrollment and a worker claim.

Fix
- kanban_placement.POOL_CARRIED_OPTIONS = ("pin_mandatory",). Pool portability skips it. The courier
  (_uncarried) still refuses it, unchanged.
- New hermes_cli/kanban_mandatory_pin.py:
  - LABEL capability:mandatory-model-pin: the attestation that a destination's core refuses a mandatory pin
    it cannot serve.
  - requirements() adds that label to a mandatory-pin card's required labels:
    - worker queue: runs_on [pool:worker, capability:mandatory-model-pin], so the Board leases it only to
      attesting machines;
    - worker-first pool: every eligible member must attest at create (placement_diagnostics), and the actual
      destination is rechecked at claim/execution (coordination.claim_refusal).
  - An old-core pool, or an unknown registry, keeps the card home with `mandatory model pin unattested`.
    Mixed versions fail closed.
  - refusal() runs on the claiming worker (runs_on_capabilities.claim_refusal). It leaves the card on the Board
    when this host cannot run the exact model/provider: no profile, family mismatch, managed registry refusal,
    provider disabled, or an unroutable class pin. It never falls back to another model.
- clear_refused_pin never clears a mandatory pin; its CAS requires model_pin_mandatory = 0.
- coordination.stranded also names a card created home for want of this attestation.
- A non-mandatory pin keeps every substitution it had: quota reroute and registry clear.

The label is published by the state repo's worker capability proof (hosts/hermes-workers/capability-proofs,
`mandatory-model-pin`, a static read of the installed core). Until it is published, mandatory-pin cards stay
on the hub exactly as before.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_mandatory_pin.py (14). On base (with a no-op stub of the new module, since
the module does not exist there) 10 fail. The 4 that pass are controls: a non-mandatory pin is not refused, a
servable pin is not refused, a mark without a model pins nothing, and the courier still refuses.
All 15 mutants of the new conditions are killed. tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_*.py: 9933 passed, 0 failed.
